# Quarteroni, Sacco and Saleri, *Numerical Mathematics*

This library formalizes the numbered results of the textbook *Numerical Mathematics* by Alfio
Quarteroni, Riccardo Sacco and Fausto Saleri [quarteroni2000numerical]. The book covers the
foundations of matrix analysis and of numerical mathematics, direct and iterative methods for linear
systems, the approximation of eigenvalues, rootfinding, nonlinear systems and optimization,
polynomial interpolation, numerical integration, approximation by orthogonal polynomials, and the
numerical solution of ordinary differential equations and of boundary and initial boundary value
problems; the library has modules for all thirteen chapters. Each module states the results of one
section in the book's own terms (real matrices, vectors of $\mathbb{R}^n$, functions on an interval
$[a, b]$, every method written as the book writes it) and gives a reader of the book a Lean name for
each result. The proofs specialize the backbone, mainly `Numlib.LinearAlgebra`, `Numlib.Analysis`,
`Numlib.Approximation`, `Numlib.Eigen`, `Numlib.Nonlinear`, `Numlib.Stationary`, `Numlib.Krylov`,
`Numlib.FiniteDifference`, `Numlib.FloatingPoint`, `Numlib.ODE` and `Numlib.Variational`, and almost
nothing is proved in the library that the backbone does not prove. This module imports all modules
of the library and adds no declaration.

## Coverage

The formalized items are the numbered definitions, theorems, properties, lemmas, corollaries,
examples, remarks and equations of each section, the exercises that the main text cites, and some
unnumbered results that the text states. Each section of the book that states a numbered result has
its own module, with two exceptions: Sections 1.1–1.6 share the module
`NumlibSurface.QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter01.Basics`, and Section 13.4 has no module, because its
only numbered result, (13.25), is not formalized. The opening of Chapter 6, before Section 6.1, has
the module `NumlibSurface.QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter06.Basics`. Sections 2.3, 2.4, 10.6 and 11.11
have no module. Out of scope are the programs, the numerical examples, tables and figures, the
operation counts, the heuristic estimates that the book writes with $\simeq$ and does not state as
propositions, the prose discussions, and the exercises that the main text does not cite. The section
`Not formalized` lists these items, and the numbered results that are not formalized, chapter by
chapter.

