  never equal $\alpha$. See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter06.theorem_6_1_limit`.
* **Equation (6.20)** (typo). The source prints $R = -\log(1/|\varphi'(\alpha)|)$; the rate is
  $R = -\log|\varphi'(\alpha)|$. See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter06.equation_6_20`.
* **Property 6.4** (typo). The source prints the range $0 \le i \le p$ for the vanishing
  derivatives; the range is $1 \le i \le p$. See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter06.property_6_4`.
* **Property 6.4** (reading). The property presupposes convergence. For $p = 0$ only the limit
  (6.21) along a convergent orbit is stated, and the limit is stated for orbits that never equal
  $\alpha$. See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter06.property_6_4`.
* **Section 6.3.1, order of Newton's method** (generalization). The order $2$ and its constant
  $f''(\alpha)/(2 f'(\alpha))$ are proved for $f \in C^2$; the derivation through Property 6.4 needs
  $f \in C^3$. See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter06.newton_order_two`.
* **Property 6.5** (reading). The "number of sign changes" of the coefficients ignores the zero
  coefficients. See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter06.property_6_5`.
* **Property 6.6** (reading). The zeros are the complex zeros of the real polynomial. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter06.property_6_6`.
* **Equation (6.28)** (reading). The divisor $g_m \in \mathbb{P}_m$ has degree exactly $m$, and the
  remainder $\rho \in \mathbb{P}_{m-1}$ has degree less than $m$. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter06.equation_6_28`.
* **Section 6.4.3, order of Muller's method** (reading). The order $p \simeq 1.84$ is the real root
  of $p^3 = p^2 + p + 1$, in the sense of Definition 6.1. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter06.muller_order`.
* **Section 6.5, residual test** (reading). The estimates are stated as limits along sequences that
  never equal $\alpha$. See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter06.residualTest` and
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter06.residualTest_simple`.
* **Equation (6.31)** (reading). The estimate is stated as a limit along an orbit that never equals
  $\alpha$. See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter06.equation_6_31`.
* **Equation (6.34)** (generalization). The source assumes linear convergence; the limit holds
  whenever $\varphi'(\alpha) \neq 1$. See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter06.equation_6_34`.
* **Equation (6.34)** (reading). The limit is stated for orbits that never equal $\alpha$. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter06.equation_6_34`.
* **Property 6.7, case $p = 1$** (reading). An iteration of order $1$ for a simple zero is read as
  $\varphi(\alpha) = \alpha$, $\varphi \in C^2$ near $\alpha$ and $\varphi'(\alpha) \neq 1$. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter06.property_6_7_one`.
* **Property 6.7, case $p \ge 2$** (generalization). The source assumes $\varphi \in C^{2p}$ near
  $\alpha$; $\varphi \in C^p$ is enough. See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter06.property_6_7_higher`.
* **Property 6.7, last clause** (correction). The source says that at a root of multiplicity
  $m \ge 2$ Aitken's method for a first-order method converges linearly with factor $1 - 1/m$. This
  is false, since $\varphi_\Delta'(\alpha) = 0$ whenever $\varphi'(\alpha) \neq 1$. The statement
  holds for Steffensen's method. See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter06.property_6_7_multiple`.

**Chapter 7. Nonlinear Systems and Numerical Optimization**

* **Property 7.1** (reading). The source states the property in the norm $\|\cdot\|_1$. Here it is
  stated in the Euclidean norm, where the error of the difference Jacobian carries a factor
  $\sqrt{n}$ that changes no conclusion. See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter07.property_7_1`.
* **Property 7.1, "or, equivalently" clause** (reading). The clause is used in one direction: a
  bound of the increments by the residual implies quadratic convergence. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter07.property_7_1_residual`.
* **Property 7.2** (reading). The source states the property without proof. It is stated on
  $\mathbb{R}^n$ with the hypotheses of Theorem 7.1, and the proof gives the constants $c_k$ of
  (7.15) explicitly. See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter07.property_7_2`.
* **Theorem 7.2** (correction). The source omits $D_0 \neq \emptyset$. The empty set satisfies the
  other hypotheses and has no fixed point. See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter07.theorem_7_2`.
* **Remark 7.1** (correction). The source writes Newton's method as $(I - J_{G_N}(x^{(k)})) \delta
  x^{(k)} = -r^{(k)}$. The matrix that makes the identity true is $J_F(x^{(k)})$. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter07.remark_7_1`.
* **Property 7.4(a)** (generalization). The source assumes $f \in C^1(B(x^*; R))$; differentiability
  at $x^*$ is enough. See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter07.property_7_4_gradient`.
* **Property 7.4(c)** (correction). The source omits the hypothesis $\nabla f(x^*) = 0$, without
  which the statement is false, and its conclusion is a strict local minimum. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter07.property_7_4_sufficient`.
* **Equation (7.31)** (reading). The source prints an outer inequality $0 \ge v_M(x^{(k+1)})$. The
  Armijo condition is the inner inequality. See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter07.equation_7_31`.
* **Property 7.8** (reading). As printed, the property is the continuity of $\nabla f$. The intended
  statement is the one for bounded sequences whose cluster points are critical. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter07.property_7_8` and
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter07.property_7_8_of_tendsto`.
* **Equations (7.45)–(7.46)** (reading). The symmetrization iteration converges to (7.46) from a
  symmetric $B_k$ only, which the source leaves implicit. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter07.equation_7_46`.
* **Section 7.2.7, bounded deterioration** (reading). The estimate needs a symmetric $B_k$, which
  the source leaves implicit. See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter07.psbBoundedDeterioration`.
* **Property 7.9(1)** (generalization). The book assumes $f$ of class $C^1$ on a ball around $x^*$;
  differentiability at $x^*$ suffices. See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter07.property_7_9_necessary`.
* **Property 7.10** (correction). The statement needs $f$ lower semicontinuous on $\Omega$. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter07.property_7_10`.
* **Property 7.11, first part** (correction). The statement needs $x^*$ regular in the sense of
  Definition 7.2: $\min x$ subject to $x^2 = 0$ has no multiplier. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter07.property_7_11`.
* **Equation (7.52)** (reading). The index ranges presuppose $l \le k$, which the book does not
  write; every statement about (7.52) assumes it. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter07.nonlinearProgram_toStandard`.
* **Property 7.13** (correction). The book mentions a constraint qualification after Property 7.14
  and does not state it; the formal statement assumes
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter07.IsRegularNLP`. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter07.property_7_13`.
* **Property 7.14** (typo). The book prints the fourth condition as
  $(\nabla_\lambda \mathcal{L})^T x^* = 0$, whose two vectors have different lengths; the formal
  statement is $(\nabla_\lambda \mathcal{L})^T \lambda^* = 0$. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter07.property_7_14`.
* **Property 7.15** (correction). The statement needs the sign conditions on the multipliers, which
  the book leaves out of its list of Kuhn–Tucker conditions. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter07.property_7_15`.
* **Property 7.15** (reading). The book's convexity assumptions "in the feasible region" are read on
  a convex set $C$ that contains the feasible region and lies in the nonnegative orthant. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter07.property_7_15`.
* **Property 7.16** (correction). The book asserts that the penalty minimizers converge to $x^*$;
  every cluster point is a constrained global minimizer, and convergence needs a unique constrained
  minimizer and a bounded sequence. See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter07.property_7_16`.
* **Example 7.7** (reading). The book says that the augmented Lagrangian "no longer admits a minimum
  at $x = 0$"; it has no global minimizer, while for $\lambda = 0$ and $\alpha > 0$ the point $0$ is
  a strict local minimizer. See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter07.example_7_7` and
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter07.example_7_7_isLocalMin`.
* **Property 7.17** (generalization). The book lists condition 3 as a hypothesis; it follows from
  condition 2 for all large $\alpha$. See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter07.property_7_17_debreu`.
* **Section 7.4.1** (generalization). The results hold for every symmetric positive definite matrix
  $A$, not only for $(\lambda/h)^2 \operatorname{tridiag}(-1, 2, -1)$; the constants $\lambda$ and
  $h$ are absorbed into $A$. See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter07.equation_7_58_existsUnique`.
* **Equation (7.58)** (typo). The book prints $\frac{1}{2} u^T A u + 2 \sum \cosh(u_i)) - b^T u$,
  with a stray parenthesis and without the factor $K$; the second term is $2K \sum \cosh(u_i)$. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter07.semiconductorFunctional`.
* **Exercise 5 of Chapter 7** (reading). The uniform convexity constant $\rho = 1/2$ is read in the
  energy norm; in the Euclidean norm of (7.49) the constant is $c/2$ for a coercivity constant $c$
  of $A$. See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter07.exercise_7_5`.

**Chapter 8. Polynomial Interpolation**

* **(8.39)** (generalization). The reference Lagrange basis of $\mathbb{P}_k(\hat T)$ is a
  hypothesis: any nodal basis with nodes in $\hat T$ that spans $\mathbb{P}_k(\hat T)$, and $h_T$ is
  any bound on the two edges at $a_1$. The constant is explicit. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter08.equation_8_39`.
* **(8.40)** (generalization). The regularity hypothesis $\max_T h_T/\rho_T \le \sigma$ of the
  source is not needed in the sup norm. See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter08.equation_8_40`.
* **(8.46), integrated form** (generalization). The source states the formula for
  $i = 1, \ldots, n - 1$. It holds on every panel. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter08.equation_8_46_integrated`.
* **The not-a-knot spline** (correction). The source does not require $n \ge 3$. For $n = 2$ the two
  conditions coincide, and the spline is not unique. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter08.notAKnotSpline_existsUnique`.
* **Property 8.3** (reading). The source does not say which end conditions it means. The statement
  is for the clamped spline; for the natural spline it is false unless $f''(a) = f''(b) = 0$. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter08.property_8_3_weak`.
* **(8.54), degree zero** (reading). The source prints the closed interval $[x_i, x_{i+1}]$ for
  $B_{i,1}$. The half-open interval $[x_i, x_{i+1})$ is used. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter08.equation_8_54_zero`.
* **Remark 8.3, coincident nodes** (typo). The source prints $f^{(n+1)}(x_0)/(n+1)!$ for
  $f[x_0, \ldots, x_0]$ with $n + 1$ nodes. The value is $f^{(n)}(x_0)/n!$. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter08.remark_8_3_confluent_const`.
* **Remark 8.3, coincident knots** (generalization). The hypotheses $x_{i-1} < x_i$ at the left and
  $x_{i+k+1} < x_{i+k+2}$ at the right are not needed. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter08.remark_8_3_left` and
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter08.remark_8_3_right`.
* **(8.55)–(8.56)** (reading). The source allows coincident fictitious knots. The basis is stated
  for strictly increasing knots. See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter08.equation_8_56`.
* **(8.57), right endpoint** (reading). With the half-open convention of (8.54), every B-spline
  vanishes at $b$, so $s_k(b) = c_{n-1}$ is stated as a left limit. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter08.equation_8_57_right`.
* **Remark 8.4, coefficients** (typo). The source prints $d_i = \omega_i c_i + (1 - \omega_i) c_i$.
  The second coefficient is $c_{i-1}$. See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter08.remark_8_4`.
* **Remark 8.4, the new knot** (reading). The source allows $\tilde x \in [x_j, x_{j+1})$. The knots
  are strictly increasing and $\tilde x$ lies in the open panel $(x_j, x_{j+1})$ of the data range.
  See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter08.remark_8_4`.
* **The parametric cubic spline** (reading). The source does not fix the end conditions of the
  coordinate splines. The natural end conditions (8.45) are used. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter08.parametricCubicSpline`.

**Chapter 9. Numerical Integration**

* **Theorem 9.3, equation (9.26)** (correction). The source prints the constant $M_n/(n + 2)^{n+3}$.
  The correct constant is $M_n/n^{n+3}$. See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter09.theorem_9_3_even`.
* **Equation (9.32)** (correction). The source prints the end correction
  $\frac{(b - a)^2}{12} [f'(a) - f'(b)]$. On $m$ panels it is $\frac{H^2}{12} [f'(a) - f'(b)]$ with
  $H = (b - a)/m$. See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter09.equation_9_32`.
* **Property 9.2** (reading). The source prints $\mathcal{A}_{m,n} = \alpha_0 + O((\delta^m
  h)^{n+1})$ for $m = 0, \dots, n$, but (9.34) defines $\mathcal{A}_{m,q}$ only for $m \ge q$. The
  library states $\mathcal{A}_{m,q} = \alpha_0 + O((\delta^m h)^{q+1})$ for $q \le m$. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter09.property_9_2`.
* **Equations (9.39)–(9.40)** (reading). The source writes an approximate equality $\simeq$ for
  every $n$. The library states it as a bound with a remainder of the next order in $H$, for the
  trapezoidal ($n = 1$) and Simpson ($n = 2$) formulae, the only composite Newton–Cotes formulae for
  which the book gives an asymptotic expansion. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter09.equation_9_40_trapezoid` and
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter09.equation_9_40_simpson`.
* **Equations (9.43)–(9.44)** (reading). The source writes an approximate equality $\simeq$. The
  library states it as a bound with a remainder controlled by the oscillation of $f^{(4)}$. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter09.equation_9_44`.
* **Equation (9.49)** (correction). The bound uses the constant $|M_n|/n^{n+3}$ of (9.26) as
  corrected in Section 9.4. See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter09.equation_9_49`.
* **The sentence after (9.52)** (generalization). The regularity of $g$ holds for every $\mu < 1$,
  without the standing assumption $0 \le \mu$ of the source. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter09.equation_9_52_regularity`.
* **Equation (9.55)** (typo). The source prints $I_2 = \int_0^{1/c} f(t) t^{-2}\,dt$; the integrand
  should be $f(1/t) t^{-2}$. See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter09.equation_9_55`.
* **Property 9.4** (generalization). The statement holds for any finite family of nondegenerate
  triangles that cover $\Omega$ and overlap only on null sets, not only for a triangulation. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter09.property_9_4`.

**Chapter 10. Orthogonal Polynomials in Approximation Theory**

* **Section 10.1, the weight function** (reading). The book leaves implicit that $w$ is not almost
  everywhere zero. The library assumes $\int_{-1}^1 w \neq 0$, without which $\|\cdot\|_w$ is not a
  norm. See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter10.isWeight_weightMeasure`.
* **Remark 10.2** (typo). The book prints the Jacobi weight as $(1 - x)^\alpha (1 - x)^\beta$. The
  library uses $(1 - x)^\alpha (1 + x)^\beta$. See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter10.remark_10_2`.
* **Remark 10.3** (typo). The book prints the map $\varphi(\xi) = \frac{a + b}{2} \xi + \frac{b -
  a}{2}$ and the factor $\frac{a + b}{2}$. The library uses $\varphi(\xi) = \frac{b - a}{2} \xi +
  \frac{a + b}{2}$ and the factor $\frac{b - a}{2}$. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter10.remark_10_3`.
* **Equations (10.30)–(10.31)** (correction). The book prints $\cos(kj\pi / n)$, which is
  $T_k(\bar x_{n-j})$ and not $T_k(\bar x_j)$ for the increasing nodes of (10.21). The library uses
  $\cos(k(n - j)\pi / n) = T_k(\bar x_j)$. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter10.chebyshevDiscreteCoeff` and
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter10.equation_10_31`.
* **Equation (10.41), the remainder** (reading). The book assumes
  $\varphi \in C^{2n}([0, +\infty))$. The library assumes that $\varphi$ is $C^{2n}$ on all of
  $\mathbb{R}$, the form in which the backbone states the Hermite interpolation error. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter10.equation_10_41_error`.
* **Equations (10.41) and (10.42), the remainders** (reading). The book leaves the convergence of
  the integral implicit. The library assumes that $e^{-x} \varphi$ is integrable on $(0, \infty)$,
  and that $e^{-x^2} \varphi$ is integrable on $\mathbb{R}$. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter10.equation_10_41_error` and
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter10.equation_10_42_error`.
* **Equation (10.46)** (generalization). The book prints $B^T B a = B^T y$, which omits the weight
  matrix that its display before (10.46) carries. The library states $B^T W B a = B^T W y$ with
  $W = \operatorname{diag}(w_j)$; the printed form is the case $w_j = 1$. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter10.equation_10_46`.
* **Exercise 10.7, essential strictness** (reading). The book asks for "nonnull $\alpha, \beta$".
  The library reads this as $(\alpha, \beta) \neq (0, 0)$, because when $f$ vanishes at all the
  nodes the pair $(1, 0)$ is the only choice. See `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter10.exercise_10_7`.
* **Section 10.7.1, linear regression** (generalization). The book defines the discrete scalar
  product $(f, g)_m$ without the weights $w_j$. The library includes the weights, as the system that
  the formulas solve does; the printed form is the case $w_j = 1$. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter10.linearRegression_coeff`.
* **Equations (10.50) and (10.52)** (typo). The book writes the coefficients as
  $(f, \tilde\varphi_k)/2\pi$ and $(f, \tilde\varphi_k)_N/2\pi$ with
  $\tilde\varphi_k = e^{-i(k - N/2)x}$, which gives the wrong sign in the exponent. The library uses
  $\tilde\varphi_k = e^{i(k - N/2)x}$. See
  `QuarteroniSaccoSaleri.Chapter10.equation_10_52_eq_discreteInner`.
* **Equation (10.55) and the matrix $C$** (correction). The book prints
  $C_{jk} = W_N^{-(j - N/2)k}$, which does not invert $T$. The library uses
